  "semantic_findings": [
    {
      "topic": "Genuine derivative and norm",
      "finding": "The expanded target is iteratedFDeriv over the real scalar field, of type ContinuousMultilinearMap (Fin k) E F, with its actual operator norm. The proof invokes the center derivative estimate for hf.changeOrigin at x+z; it is not merely a coefficient bound or bound on diagonal polynomial evaluations."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Positive radius and buffer",
      "finding": "The local radius theorem uses s>0 and r+s<R<=p.radius. For each ||z||<=r this proves ||z||<R, so changeOrigin is applied within the actual expansion ball. AnalyticAt supplies a positive expansion radius; a positive finite t<R is selected and split into r=s=t/2. Hence rho=r>0 and A=1/r>0 are legitimate. Infinite expansion radius and k=0 cause no exception."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Uniform local estimate",
      "finding": "The single translated-series majorant C is chosen before all z and all orders k. The center inequality introduces exactly k!, and C/s^k is rewritten as C*A^k. No finite-order compactness argument or order-dependent hidden constant is used in the two reviewed modules."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Compact specialization",
      "finding": "The only mathematical hypotheses of the final theorem are a real normed domain, a complete real normed range, compact K, and AnalyticOnNhd of f on K. Its hlocal input is supplied by analyticAt_local_factorial_bound at each point. No local factorial estimate or derivative bound is assumed by the final statement."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Common constants and empty set",
      "finding": "The finite-cover assembly chooses U,C,A before all y in U and all orders, with C,A>=1. The K corollary restricts the same bound by subset_of_mem_nhdsSet. No nonempty K assumption is used; the empty finite cover has constants 1 and vacuous estimates."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Scope limitations",
      "finding": "The constants may depend on f and K; the theorem does not make them uniform over an external family. It proves analytic functions have factorial derivative bounds, not that a Coulomb/KS solution is analytic. Classical selection and real power-series data do not furnish effective constant extraction or a bit-complexity theorem."
    }
